<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">East Lothian</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothian">Lothian</a> is a council area of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> on the southern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Firth of Forth</a>, with Haddington as its historic county town. It contains the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> hillfort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/traprain-law">Traprain Law</a>, a centre of the Votadini, and the site of the Battle of Prestonpans of 1745.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/east-lothian.jpg" alt="East Lothian" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:East_Lothian_in_Scotland.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothian">Lothian</a> lies south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Firth of Forth</a> in the eastern central Lowlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> to the west and the Scottish Borders to the south. It is predominantly rural, with 40 miles of coastline along which lie Musselburgh, Prestonpans, North Berwick and Dunbar. Off the coast north of North Berwick are small islands, the largest being Fidra, Lamb, Craigleith and Bass Rock. To the south the Lammermuir Hills mark the boundary with Berwickshire. Its county town is Haddington and its largest town is Musselburgh.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the end of Roman occupation, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothian">Lothian</a> was populated by Brythonic-speaking Britons and formed part of the kingdom of the Gododdin. In the 7th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a> took it, and it became part of Bernicia, then of Northumbria. It came under the Scottish monarchy in the 10th century. Haddington, Dunbar and North Berwick became royal burghs, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II</a> was born at the Palace of Haddington in 1198.</p><p>The county lay on the route of repeated wars between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. Edward I's forces won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dunbar-1296">Battle of Dunbar</a> in 1296, and in 1650 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>'s Parliamentary forces defeated Covenanter forces at another <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dunbar-1650">Battle of Dunbar</a>. In September 1745 the Jacobites under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-edward-stuart">Charles Edward Stuart</a> won a significant victory over Sir John Cope at the Battle of Prestonpans, before their defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-culloden">Culloden</a> in April 1746.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The county was called Haddingtonshire until 1921, when its name was changed to East Lothian. It became a district of Lothian Region in 1975 and one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>'s 32 council areas in 1996. Its economy has long been agricultural, with fishing and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>-mining also significant, and Dunbar, Tantallon and Dirleton castles date from its medieval and early modern conflicts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “East Lothian” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>An English army under John de Warenne, Earl of Surrey, routed the Scots near Dunbar in <b>East Lothian</b> after Edward I's invasion and sack of Berwick.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-27">27 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>They descended from Iron Age communities of Lothian and the Borders, and their great hillfort at Traprain Law in <b>East Lothian</b> was a major centre from before the Roman arrival in the 80s.</blockquote><a href="/state/votadini">Votadini</a></li></ul></section>
